+## 233. IAU 2000B takes the full Delaunay polynomials to T^4 where the published model takes their linear terms: 7.4e-9 deg off it at 2023, 1.6e-7 at 2100, 1.4e-5 at AD 1000, 1.5e-4 at -1000
+
+**Severity: Correctness (the nutation under SEMOD_NUT_IAU_2000B, in this fork set explicitly, upstream's default: off the published model by 5.9e-10 deg at 2006, 7.4e-9 at 2023 and more away from 2000; both builds)**
+**Where:** `swephlib.c`: `calc_nutation_iau2000ab()`, `calc_nutation_2000b_rate()`, new `nut_ls_args_linear_exact()`
+
+Entry 220's noticed item. IAU 2000B (McCarthy & Luzum 2003, Celest. Mech.
+Dyn. Astron. 85, 37), as SOFA's `iauNut00b` and NOVAS's `iau2000b`
+compute it, is the 77 luni-solar terms on the Delaunay arguments' linear
+terms (Simon et al. 1994's constants and rates: `el = fmod(485868.249036
++ 1717915923.2178 * t, TURNAS)`, and so on) and the fixed offsets of entry
+220. The library's 2000B was its 2000A function with the loop cut short:
+the arguments' full polynomials to T^4. Measured on Moshier, SE_ECL_NUT
+under "0,0,0,4" against the published model in mpmath at 300 bits (the 77
+terms of swenut2000a.h on the linear arguments, the offsets; it
+reproduces SOFA's test values to 5e-24 rad), dpsi and deps, deg:
+5.9e-10 and 5.1e-11 at 2006 Jan 1, 7.4e-9 and 2.7e-9 at 2023, 2.9e-8 and
+9.1e-8 at 1900, 1.6e-7 and 1.5e-8 at 2100, 1.4e-5 and 3.7e-6 at AD 1000,
+1.5e-4 and 1.3e-5 at -1000; against SOFA's test values at TT 2453736.5
+(t_sofa_c.c) 1.02e-11 rad in dpsi. Both builds (the compat build without
+the offsets too).
+
+What a user of 2000B gains and loses: against the library's IAU 2000A
+(the direct series), SE_ECL_NUT at 1500 random instants a span, mas, dpsi
+worst (rms), deps worst:
+
+| years | the full polynomials | the linear terms (published) |
+|---|---|---|
+| -3000..-1000 | 3.70 (1.52), 2.33 | 1440 (666), 774 |
+| -1000..1000 | 3.59 (0.97), 1.92 | 537 (208), 293 |
+| 1000..1800 | 2.01 (0.64), 1.41 | 62.3 (21.4), 32.4 |
+| 1800..1995 | 2.71 (0.61), 1.12 | 3.41 (1.03), 2.03 |
+| 1995..2050 | 2.01 (0.53), 0.69 | 2.06 (0.53), 0.72 |
+| 2050..2200 | 2.06 (0.52), 1.10 | 3.50 (1.07), 2.05 |
+| 2200..3000 | 2.32 (0.63), 1.40 | 65.1 (22.0), 34.3 |
+
+So the published model holds its stated milliarcsecond over 1995-2050 as
+the polynomials do, and away from it is its own approximation, off 2000A
+by up to 1.44" at -3000; the library's 2000B was the better long-range
+stand-in for 2000A, under a name that says the published model. The Swiss
+Ephemeris documentation gives no reason for the polynomials and claims
+nothing beyond the model: "Nutation IAU 2000B (could be switched to 2000A
+by the user)" (swisseph.doc 3), "the nutation model IAU2000B (can be
+switched to IAU2000A)" (swephprg, changes to 1.70), "fast, but precision
+of milli-arcsec" (swephexp.h); this fork's default is 2000A on its grid,
+fast and the full model. 2000B is the published model now, as entry 220
+made its offsets; a caller after 2000A's accuracy far from 2000 has the
+default.
+
+**Fix, as done here:** under SEMOD_NUT_IAU_2000B `calc_nutation_iau2000ab()`
+takes the arguments' linear terms, past 4e8 days in double-double
+(`nut_ls_args_linear_exact()`, entry 188's table to its T term), and
+`calc_nutation_2000b_rate()` the same arguments, their rates the constant
+linear terms, so the rate stays the places' derivative. IAU 2000A and the
+default unchanged, bit for bit (entry 231's comparison). Behind `#ifndef
+SWE_UPSTREAM_COMPAT`: the compat build's default keeps upstream's values.
+- After: within 2.5e-15 deg of the published model at -1000..3500, 9.5e-15
+  at AD 1000; SOFA's test values to 1.1e-19 and 7.9e-21 rad.
+- G140's long double reference for 2000B (check (c)) takes the linear
+  arguments: 1.58e-13 deg (0.205 deg off its full-polynomial reference at
+  1e9 days, where the T^4 terms tell). G1's `cov:nutmodel[4]` (J2000,
+  T = 0) does not move; G139's rows under "0,8,8,4" move within their
+  limits.
+
+G162 `tests/nutseries.c`, check (c): against the published model in
+mpmath (the table regenerated) within 1e-13 deg, measured 2.5e-15, and
+SOFA's test values within 1e-17 rad (was 3e-11), measured 1.07e-19.
+Check (f) under 2000B: 1.6e-14 and 3.1e-14 deg/day (1.6e-10 and 2.5e-10
+before the fix, the five-point difference's own truncation as the T^4
+terms turned the arguments some ten times faster at 1e9 days). G162
+passes 89 of 89; before the fixes of 232 and 233 it fails 10, (c)'s 2
+(1.51e-4 deg, 1.02e-11 rad), (e)'s 4 and (f)'s 4; the upstream build 81 of
